{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "Fully pipelined, 1 result/cycle, one register per micro-rotation: the highest-Fmax family I own, so it clears the burst-latency constraint (N+2+31 cycles inside 0.5 us) even at large N. It anchors the high-accuracy / high-Fmax end of the front and is the safe fallback if pipelined_m cannot close timing at any m. Its cost is one register bank per stage, so it will be area-dominated by pipelined_m at equal accuracy; I still need it to bound the front and to keep a feasible design if the batch bound bites."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "Same bit-exact accuracy as pipelined for the same (W, N, guards, rounding) but registers only every m stages, so it trades register area against Fmax: larger m means fewer FFs (lower area) but a longer combinational path and lower Fmax, which pushes the batch latency (ceil(N/m)+2+31)/Fmax up. Since the objective is min area and the selection rule is min area, this is the family most likely to own the low-area end of the front; m 2..8 is the main structural lever and must be swept fully to find the largest m that still meets sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5. I give it the larger share because the front is expected to be dominated by it, with pipelined only as the timing-safe corner."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Both families I own are fully pipelined and emit 1 result/cycle, so the throughput_msps >= 32 constraint is met at any plausible Fmax and is not the discriminator. The two binding constraints are accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) and the system burst bound (32 requests issued at one 1 MHz tick, all back within 0.5 us). Accuracy depends only on W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding, so I search the accuracy-relevant ranges once and let both families share them: W from 13 (smallest width whose output LSB 2^-(W-2) can reach 2^-12 with rounding; W=12 cannot) to 20, N from 10 (below the ~13-iteration accuracy threshold, to let the optimiser locate the feasibility boundary) to 20, angle_guard -2..4 and frac_guard 0..4 to let the optimiser trim angle-path and datapath widths, and both rounding modes. The burst bound is what separates the families: pipelined latency is (N+2+31)/Fmax, pipelined_m is (ceil(N/m)+2+31)/Fmax, so m is a genuine area-vs-Fmax-vs-latency knob and is swept 2..8. The Pareto front is therefore area_um2 vs accuracy_bits, with the min-area feasible point sitting where accuracy just clears 12 bits and m is as large as the 0.5 us batch bound allows. I weight pipelined_m 0.6 because it should dominate the low-area end, and keep pipelined at 0.4 as the high-Fmax anchor and fallback. I keep the first round to a subset of the 100-eval share so later rounds can refine the accuracy-feasibility boundary and the largest feasible m; code's reserved mapping pass should then cover the full ranges of whichever of these two families lands on the front."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "Final round: the registry is not infeasible — round 1 produced a feasible Pareto front of 7 designs (8 unique feasible of 60 evals), all satisfying the three constraints. The min-area corner the selection rule picks (pipelined, data_width=17, n_iter=17, angle_guard=4, frac_guard=0, round) is 48071 um^2 at 12.7 accuracy bits, 128 MSPS throughput (>= 32 required) and 2^-12.74 max error (<= 2^-12 required), and the front's best sys_p99_batch_us seen is 0.293 us (<= 0.5 required), so the constraint set is reachable with margin. The front already spans the accuracy objective well (12.7..17.1 bits vs HV ref 12) and area 48071..72132 vs HV ref 100000, i.e. it covers the useful trade-off region; pipelined_m contributed 0 feasible designs (max 78.8 MSPS, best 15.92 bits) and no unexplored family remains, so further exploration would not change the selection outcome. Stopping with the front as mapped."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
